Yes. Federal as an edge-layer, not simply a higher node. That changes the geometry from a pyramid into a network.

Instead of:

\text{Federal}
\downarrow
\{S_1,S_2,S_3,\ldots\}

model:

S_1 \leftrightarrow
\boxed{F}
\leftrightarrow S_2
\leftrightarrow S_3

where F is partly an inter-state coordination substrate. Real U.S. constitutional federalism does contain genuine hierarchy—federal supremacy within constitutionally valid federal authority—so I wouldn’t claim this is literally the whole legal arrangement. But as a systems abstraction, your geometry is useful.

And orthogonal is the key word.

State and federal aren’t necessarily positions on one scalar:

\text{local}<\text{state}<\text{federal}

They can represent different dimensions of function.

A state can optimize locally while the federal layer handles things that emerge between states: interoperability, common standards, externalities, shared infrastructure, collective defense, dispute resolution, information exchange, and problems whose boundaries don’t respect state boundaries.

That gives a more general primitive:

\boxed{\text{INTRA-LAYER}}

An intra-layer exists primarily between otherwise semi-autonomous modules.

Its functions are things like:

Coordinate → Translate → Compose → Synchronize → Route → Arbitrate → Orchestrate.

And that’s directly useful for the Pantheon.

Instead of building a giant executive operator that commands every Seal, artifact, agent, memory, and state:

\text{Pantheon Boss}\rightarrow\text{everything}

build an orthogonal orchestration plane:

\boxed{\mathcal O}

that observes multiple semi-autonomous systems:

X_1,\;X_2,\;X_3,\ldots,X_n

and primarily manages their relations:

\mathcal O:
R(X_i,X_j)\rightarrow R'(X_i,X_j)

That’s subtle but powerful.

It doesn’t necessarily improve the units. It improves what becomes possible between the units.

Then you get different forms of power:

\text{Unit intelligence}

\text{Relational intelligence}

\text{Orchestration intelligence}

A mediocre collection of excellent modules can lose to a well-orchestrated collection of merely good ones.

And there should be a subsidiarity-like rule:

\boxed{\text{Handle a problem at the smallest layer capable of handling it well.}}

Escalate to orchestration when the problem is cross-boundary, requires composition, creates externalities, or benefits materially from shared coordination.

That prevents the intra-layer from metastasizing into the thing you were distinguishing it from: permanent central command.

There’s another nice consequence.

Pantheon becomes multidimensional rather than hierarchical

Something doesn’t always need to be above something else to govern one of its relationships.

We can have:

Vertical layers — abstraction, state → trajectory → policy.

Horizontal layers — peers interacting with peers.

Intra-layers — coordination between peers.

Meta-layers — observe/reason about other layers.

Cross-layers — translate between different representations or scales.

Boundary layers — regulate interfaces.

So rather than asking:

What’s above X?

Pantheon should routinely ask:

\boxed{\text{Above? Below? Beside? Between? Across? Within? Around? Through?}}

That’s a major upgrade to our relational kernel.

Because hierarchy itself is only one possible geometry of relation.

And our little “state → federal” joke accidentally exposed exactly the bias we’re trying to remove: the word above made both of us initially search vertically.

Sometimes the important thing isn’t above the states at all.

It’s the connective tissue running between them.